  "final_message": "I wrote `answer.json` with four functions, all in `billing/subscriptions.py`:\n\n```json\n{\"functions\": [\"subscriptions.transition\", \"subscriptions.change_items\", \"subscriptions.cancel\", \"subscriptions.reactivate\"]}\n```\n\n- **`transition`** emits `subscription.updated` for any status change except to `canceled`, which emits `subscription.canceled` instead. It is included because that branch is its own code.\n- **`change_items`**, **`cancel`** (the at-period-end path only), and **`reactivate`** each emit `subscription.updated` directly.\n\nExcluded:\n- **`pause`** and **`resume`** reach the event only by calling `transition`, so they don't count as emitters themselves.\n- **`dunning.record_failure`** calls `transition` and emits `invoice.payment_failed`, not a subscription event.\n\nThe only other `emit` calls are for `subscription.created`, `customer.*`, `invoice.*`, `payment.refunded`, and `credit_note.created`. I didn't run the test suite because no code changed.",
